import Mathlib

/-! # Carmichael helper — the prime-index case

This file supplies the prime-index half of Carmichael's primitive-divisor theorem
for Fibonacci numbers, which the composite-case development
(`Shared.CarmichaelProof`, `Speculative.AutoResearch.CarmichaelComposite`) imports.

For a **prime** index `p ≥ 13`, *every* prime divisor of `F p` is automatically a
primitive prime divisor: its Fibonacci entry point divides `p`, hence is `1` or `p`;
it cannot be `1` (that would force the prime to divide `F 1 = 1`), so it is exactly
`p`.  No size estimate is needed — this is the easy, "local data is forced by the
prime poset" half of Carmichael's theorem.

-- !-- Lab Notebook -- !--
Hypothesis : For prime `n`, any prime factor of `F n` is primitive.
Result     : Confirmed, `sorry = 0`.  The gcd-collapse `F (gcd n k) = gcd (F n) (F k)`
             (`Nat.fib_gcd`) reduces a hypothetical earlier appearance at `k` to an
             appearance at `gcd n k ∣ n`; primality of `n` pins `gcd n k ∈ {1, n}`,
             and `gcd = 1` is impossible since `F 1 = 1`.
Insight    : Primitivity at a prime index is purely order-theoretic — the divisor
             lattice of a prime is the two-element chain `{1, p}`, so "absent below"
             is free.  The genuine difficulty of Carmichael lives entirely in the
             composite case (size/cyclotomic estimates).
Failure    : None for the prime case; the composite tail (`n > 10000`) is the deep
             open frontier left in `Shared.CarmichaelProof`.
-- !-- End Lab Notebook -- !--
-/

/-- `F n > 1` whenever `3 ≤ n`. -/
theorem fib_one_lt_of_three_le {n : ℕ} (hn : 3 ≤ n) : 1 < Nat.fib n := by
  have h1 : Nat.fib 3 ≤ Nat.fib n := Nat.fib_mono hn
  simpa using lt_of_lt_of_le (by decide : (1 : ℕ) < Nat.fib 3) h1

/-- **Carmichael's theorem, prime-index case.**  For a prime `n ≥ 13`, the Fibonacci
number `F n` has a primitive prime divisor: a prime `p` with `p ∣ F n` but
`p ∤ F k` for every `0 < k < n`.  (In fact this holds for every prime `n ≥ 3`; we
state it at `13` to match the composite-case interface.) -/
theorem fib_primitive_divisor_prime (n : ℕ) (hn : 13 ≤ n) (hnp : Nat.Prime n) :
    ∃ p, Nat.Prime p ∧ p ∣ Nat.fib n ∧
      ∀ k, 0 < k → k < n → ¬(p ∣ Nat.fib k) := by
  have hfn : 1 < Nat.fib n := fib_one_lt_of_three_le (by omega)
  refine ⟨(Nat.fib n).minFac, Nat.minFac_prime (by omega),
    Nat.minFac_dvd _, ?_⟩
  intro k hk hkn hpk
  -- the minimal prime factor `p` divides `F (gcd n k)`
  have hpn : (Nat.fib n).minFac ∣ Nat.fib n := Nat.minFac_dvd _
  have hgcd : (Nat.fib n).minFac ∣ Nat.fib (Nat.gcd n k) := by
    rw [Nat.fib_gcd]; exact Nat.dvd_gcd hpn hpk
  -- `gcd n k` divides the prime `n`, and is `< n`, hence equals `1`
  have hdvd : Nat.gcd n k ∣ n := Nat.gcd_dvd_left n k
  have hlt : Nat.gcd n k < n := lt_of_le_of_lt (Nat.gcd_le_right n hk) hkn
  have : Nat.gcd n k = 1 := by
    rcases (Nat.Prime.eq_one_or_self_of_dvd hnp _ hdvd) with h | h
    · exact h
    · omega
  rw [this] at hgcd
  simp at hgcd
  omega
